sentence dot stands about 100 mm from its text, right beside the picture dot) and G2 (minor: the
key says "picture 2", but the pictures carry no numbers).

## What the role wrote

One test file: `dashboard/__tests__/storytime-print-review-more.test.ts` (the file of the P9-B
batch, as the plan task asks). No production code changed.

New cases, with the mutants named in the header of the file (M1 the sentence dot at the old place,
M2 a gap below 40 mm, M3 the key says "picture N"):

- P9C-dot: on the compiled pages 134, 136, 138, and 140, the sentence dot of each of the 4 match
  rows stands with its left edge 2 mm or less after the right edge of the printed sentence text.
- P9C-gap: in each match row, the horizontal gap between the sentence dot and the picture dot is
  40 mm or more. The case also keeps the form of P9-B9: the picture dot stands at the left edge of
  its picture box (the shipped gap is 2.0 mm; the case holds 3.0 mm or less and forbids the dot
  inside the box).
- P9C-key: the answer key of `manual.ts` gives each pair as "<text> = the picture in row <k>",
  where k is 1 to 4 the row of the matching picture box counted from the top. The case asserts the
  16 exact English lines of the P9-B10 texts with the default pictureOrder of P9-B10 (day 2
  [2, 0, 3, 1], day 3 [1, 3, 0, 2], day 4 [3, 2, 1, 0], day 5 [1, 0, 3, 2]), the 4 unchanged read
  sentences, and that no line holds "picture <N>".

The derivation of k: the row j of the page shows the picture of the pair `pictureOrder[j]`, so the
row of a pair is `pictureOrder.indexOf(pairIndex) + 1`. The derivation of each day is written in
the comment of `KEY_PINS` in the test file.

The positions come from the rendered page, not from CSS text: Chrome lays the compiled student book
out, and the boxes of the rows are read in mm (the C6 method of the track). The tolerance of a
measure is 0.3 mm, as in C2-book.

